Find a program on PATH on Windows when it is named without `.exe`.

cosmo 4.0.2's commandv() (behind execvp, execlp, execvpe, posix_spawnp)
tries each PATH directory with the name exactly as given and nothing
else. A Windows program is a file called `where.exe`, so `where`, `cmd`
or `git` are never found: ENOENT before CreateProcess is reached. That is
how findutils' `xargs where` failed on Windows, and why `xargs` with no
command could not run `echo`.

Upstream removed its extension guessing in c88f95a89 (2023), reasoning
that CreateProcess appends `.exe` itself. It does, but commandv() first
checks access() on the bare path, which does not exist, so the search
never gets that far.

On Windows, a name whose last component has no dot is now also tried
with `.com` and `.exe`, in that order, in each directory (cmd.exe's
default PATHEXT, minus `.bat`/`.cmd`, which CreateProcess cannot run).
Other platforms, and names that already have an extension, are
untouched.

Verified on a Windows 10 VM: `where`, `cmd`, `notepad` resolve to
`/C/Windows/system32/<name>.exe`, `posix_spawnp("where")` and
`execlp("hostname")` run, `nosuch` and `foo.bar` stay ENOENT; on Linux
`sh` and `ls` resolve as before.

--- a/libc/calls/commandv.c
+++ b/libc/calls/commandv.c
@@ -18,6 +18,7 @@
 ╚─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────*/
 #include "libc/calls/calls.h"
 #include "libc/calls/struct/stat.h"
+#include "libc/dce.h"
 #include "libc/errno.h"
 #include "libc/intrin/strace.h"
 #include "libc/paths.h"
@@ -53,6 +54,15 @@
     syspath = _PATH_DEFPATH;
